====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[have]] on hand for [[sale]].
#: {{ux|en|The store '''stocks''' all kinds of dried vegetables.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=William Froug|title=How I Escaped from Gilligan's Island|passage=...he would not '''stock''' any product on his shelves from any company that hired a communist or, as it was called at the time, a comsymp.}}
# To provide with material requisites; to store; to fill; to supply.
#: {{ux|en|to '''stock''' a warehouse with goods}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''stock''' a farm, i.e. to supply it with cattle and tools}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''stock''' land, i.e. to occupy it with a permanent growth, especially of grass}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=February|author=K. Westcott Jones|title=Some Australian Railway Byways|journal=Railway Magazine|page=118|text=A rather interesting and notable convenience, however, is that of ice water bags, which are hung on to the outside of the coaches at certain stops. These can be reached by leaning out of the window rather perilously, to unhook them, and paper cups are '''stocked''' in the compartments.}}
# To allow (cows) to retain milk for twenty-four hours or more prior to sale.
# To put in the [[stocks]] as punishment.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|passage=Poor Tom, that{{...}}eats cow-dung for sallets; swallows the old rat, and the ditch-dog; drinks the green mantle of the standing pool; who is whipp'd from tything to tything, and '''stock'd''', punish'd, and imprison'd}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To fit (an anchor) with a stock, or to fasten the stock firmly in place.
# {{lb|en|card games|dated}} To arrange cards in a certain manner for cheating purposes; to stack the deck.

